// =======================================================
// std/term.cst
// Terminal: raw mode, ANSI escapes, event-based input
// =======================================================
//
// Output — colours, cursor, clear — writes bytes to STDOUT and touches nothing
// else. Input is a buffered reader that owns the descriptor and hands out one
// Event at a time; read_key() is a shell over a reader of exactly that kind —
// this module's own, shared_input() — and still returns one i32, so nothing that
// used it had to change.
//
// Why the parse and the reading were separated
// --------------------------------------------
// The old reader read ONE byte per call and decided what it had from the third
// byte. On a sequence it did not recognise it returned KEY_ESC having already
// consumed three bytes, and the rest of the sequence stayed in the stream and
// came back as loose keystrokes. A mouse report is `ESC [ < 0 ; 40 ; 12 M`:
// byte three is `<`, no branch matched, three bytes were gone, and `0;40;12M`
// was typed into whatever had focus. Nothing could be layered on top of that,
// because reading and parsing were the same loop.
//
// They are two things now. _scan() is a pure function over a byte range that
// either produces one event or says "these bytes are a prefix, feed me more";
// the reader is the only code here that talks to a descriptor. Every introducer
// is walked to its own terminator and consumed as a unit whether or not
// anything recognises it: CSI (ESC [) and SS3 (ESC O) to a final byte
// 0x40..0x7E, SS2 (ESC N) over its one payload byte, and the string types —
// OSC (ESC ]), DCS (ESC P), SOS (ESC X), PM (ESC ^), APC (ESC _) — to an ST
// (ESC \) or, for OSC, a BEL.
//
// The five string introducers are in that list because leaving them out was a
// measured defect and not a theoretical one. A 25-byte OSC 11 reply, which some
// terminals send unasked, arrived as Escape plus its own text — 23 keystrokes,
// the first of them the one that opens a menu in the interface built on this.
//
// An unknown sequence is reported as nothing — EV_NONE, KEY_NONE — and not as
// Escape. An escape sequence we do not understand is not the Escape key, and
// answering Escape is how a terminal's unsolicited replies became menu
// toggles. A bare ESC with nothing behind it is still KEY_ESC, which is the one
// Escape behaviour every consumer of this module depends on.
//
// Where "nothing behind" is not the whole truth, and both cases are deliberate:
//
//   - `ESC [` and `ESC O` with nothing behind them still answer KEY_ESC. That
//     is what the old reader answered and consumers were built against it. The
//     newer introducers do NOT: a truncated `ESC ]` or `ESC N` answers nothing,
//     because inventing an Escape is the harm this module was written to stop.
//   - a string sequence with no terminator, once nothing more is coming,
//     gives up its two introducer bytes and leaves its payload to be read as
//     keystrokes. Those two bytes are also exactly what Alt+] or Alt+P arrive
//     as, and no terminator ever follows those, so swallowing the payload would
//     eat what the person typed next. A terminal writes a reply in one write,
//     so a real reply does not get split across that window; a person pressing
//     Alt and a letter is not rare at all.
//
// What read_key() still promises
// ------------------------------
// It reads one byte per read(2) and answers on the first sequence it finishes —
// a keystroke as its i32, a report as KEY_NONE — so the rest of a burst stays in
// the kernel queue where a level-triggered poller can still see it, and its read
// blocks: VMIN=0 with VTIME=1 bounds that at about 100 ms, and that timeout is
// the only thing that can tell a bare Escape from the start of an arrow key. All
// three are load bearing for a caller that polls fd 0 itself and calls read_key
// once per readiness report; see the note on input_fd below for what changes
// when a caller moves to events instead, and read_key's own header for the three
// places where "one sequence per call" needs a qualification.
//
// One answer is not the byte it came from. The NUL byte — Ctrl+@, Ctrl+Space —
// cannot be answered as 0, because 0 is KEY_NONE and KEY_NONE is what this
// function says whenever there is nothing to report; a caller guarding on it
// dropped the keystroke. read_key answers KEY_NUL for it. The event API is
// untouched and needs to be: there the byte is EV_KEY with key=0, and a kind
// plus a key can say what one i32 cannot.
//
// Two answers changed, both of them where the old ones were wrong. A sequence
// that is not a key — a mouse report, a paste bracket, a reply the terminal
// sent unasked — is consumed whole and answered KEY_NONE instead of KEY_ESC
// plus its own tail as text. And ESC immediately followed by ESC no longer eats
// whatever came after it: the byte behind the second ESC decides. If it
// introduces a sequence, only the first ESC is consumed and the second is left
// to introduce it; if it does not, the two ESCs are consumed as one Escape and
// the byte itself is untouched. Both readings destroy input if applied always —
// consuming one always loses the byte after it (`ESC ESC x` answered Escape,
// Escape and no x), consuming both always loses a sequence the second ESC was
// introducing (a paste's `[200~` typed into the input).
//
// That decision needs the third byte, so read_key can end a call holding an ESC
// ESC pair's worth of bytes in this module's buffer rather than in the kernel
// queue. A level-triggered poller cannot see those, so they are delivered by
// the next read_key call — which, in a loop that only calls read_key when the
// descriptor says it is readable, is the next time any input arrives. That is
// the price of not guessing, and it is one byte, never a lost one.
//
// A paste is NOT among the things read_key answers KEY_NONE to. Bracketed text
// is not the terminal talking, it is a person putting two hundred characters
// into the program, and an event reader that eats them because its caller asked
// for an i32 is worse than the defect this module was written to remove. So
// read_key does not gather a paste: it consumes the brackets, reports them as
// nothing, and delivers the bytes between them as the keystrokes they would
// have been if typed — one per call, in order, each still taken one byte per
// read(2) so a level-triggered poller keeps seeing the rest. What is lost by
// that choice is the knowledge that they were pasted: an ESC inside pasted text
// is read as an escape sequence, because an i32 cannot say otherwise. A caller
// that needs the distinction calls input_next, which does gather it.
//
// Who owns the reader read_key reads
// -----------------------------------
// This module does. read_key() takes no arguments and is called from code that
// does not know Input exists, so the reader it reads has to be one whose
// lifetime nothing outside can end: shared_input(), in .bss. An earlier version
// kept a module-scope pointer to whatever Input was last handed to input_open,
// which is a pointer into the caller's memory — a stack-local Input whose frame
// had returned left it live, and the next read_key read and WROTE 12 KB through
// it. A program that wants both read_key and input_next against ONE reader hands
// shared_input() to input_open.
//
// What input_close puts back, and what nothing puts back
// ------------------------------------------------------
// input_close restores every mode input_open set and only those, in the reverse
// order, each gated on this reader having actually set it — and a second
// input_open does not erase that record, it amends it. That is worth stating
// because getting it wrong is not cosmetic: mouse reporting left on leaves the
// shell that started the program printing `<35;40;12M` at every click, and
// bracketed paste left on leaves it printing `[200~` around every paste.
//
// A program that dies WITHOUT reaching input_close leaves all five modes set,
// and nothing here can prevent that. There is no sigaction wrapper anywhere in
// this stdlib, so there is no handler to hang cleanup off; a SIGTERM, a
// segfault, or a kill leaves the terminal reporting mouse and bracketing pastes
// until the user runs `reset`, with nothing to tell them that is what they need.
// input_open introduces that risk — the old reader set no global terminal modes
// at all — and this paragraph is here instead of a claim that close is enough.
//
// The numbers
// -----------
// IN_BUF is 4096 because that is the size of the kernel's tty line-discipline
// buffer on Linux (N_TTY_BUF_SIZE): one read of that size empties a queue as
// full as the kernel will ever let it get. IN_PASTE (8192) and _RK_MAX (64)
// were chosen, not derived; each says at its declaration what it is measured
// against.
//
// A coordinate is bounded twice. _COORD_MAX (65535) is derived — a winsize
// carries rows and columns as u16 — and is all the pure parser can do; on top of
// it the reader asks the descriptor for its actual size and drops a report for a
// cell the terminal does not have, at 377 ns per delivered report (measured over
// a million TIOCGWINSZ calls on a real pty). A parameter is bounded at nine
// digits, which is where the accumulator cannot overflow. Both exist because the
// bytes come from outside the process and a caller indexes a grid with what
// comes out: an unbounded accumulator delivered col=7766279631452241919 out of
// twenty digits, positive and past every guard.
//
// A parameter past that digit bound refuses the WHOLE sequence, wherever in a
// sequence it sits: a mouse report with a twenty-digit column produces no
// coordinate, and `ESC [ 1 ; 9999999999 A` produces no arrow. It used to produce
// one with no modifier — "there is no digit here" and "these digits do not fit"
// were the same -1, and a modifier has a default for the first of them while a
// coordinate has none.
//
// A sequence longer than any of those is not a terminal talking — the longest
// this parser recognises is a 25-byte OSC reply — so it is SKIPPED rather than
// dropped: the reader remembers which terminator it is looking for and discards
// bytes until that terminator arrives. Dropping the buffer instead is what let
// a 6000-byte CSI hand 1906 bytes back as keystrokes, which is how a hostile
// writer on fd 0 types arbitrary text into whatever has focus.
//
// EV_RESIZE is not implemented
// ----------------------------
// The design lists it at 4. Nothing here can deliver it: a resize is SIGWINCH,
// there is no sigaction in this stdlib at all, and the one interface built on
// this module has no signal handler either — it re-polls TIOCGWINSZ once a
// frame through get_size(), about ten ioctls a second, and detects resizes that
// way. The two ways to fake the event are both worse than what already works:
// an ioctl on every input_next() call would be more ioctls than the frame poll —
// the one this reader does make is per delivered MOUSE report, and only while
// mouse mode is on — and it would only fire when a keystroke happened to arrive. So the event does not exist,
// 4 is left unused so it can be added without renumbering, and get_size()
// stays a standalone ioctl with no Input anywhere near it.
//
// Portability, honestly
// ---------------------
// Windows with ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_INPUT — which enter_raw() already sets —
// delivers VT input sequences, so one SGR-1006 parser should serve both
// targets, and the mode strings input_open() writes are bytes the console
// understands once ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_PROCESSING is on. Should.
//
// NONE OF THE WINDOWS INPUT PATH HAS BEEN RUN. No Windows machine was in
// reach, and this comment is here instead of a claim that it works. Three things
// specifically are unverified: whether the console delivers SGR mouse reports
// through ReadFile at all; _pending()'s use of WaitForSingleObject on the console
// input handle — that handle signals when an input RECORD is available, and a
// key-up or a buffer-resize record is a record with no bytes behind it, which
// would make _pending() answer yes and the read that follows block; and
// _size_of(), which the mouse gate calls — on Windows it asks about the console
// the process is attached to rather than about the reader's descriptor, because
// there the size is a property of the output handle.
//
// What was run
// ------------
// tests/term_input_test.cst at -O0/-O1/-O2: 382 assertions, the parser called
// directly and the reader driven over a pipe. Every public KEY_* value is pinned
// there by number, because consumers were compiled against the numbers even
// though they compare by name.
//
// Three things this suite named and did not actually check, each found by
// mutating the code and watching every assertion stay green, and each now pinned
// by a mutation that dies: the SGR button ceiling (deleting `cb > _BUTTON_MAX`
// used to break nothing; six assertions fail now, and `<300;5;6M` used to arrive
// as an event with motion and two modifier bits the report never carried); the
// five bytes a paste holds back when its closing bracket may be split (the old
// case wrote two write(2)s that a pipe fused into ONE read, so the hold-back
// never ran — the case now ends a read exactly at IN_BUF, and `stop = in.tail`
// makes the paste come out 4093 bytes instead of 4087); and read_key's handover
// of a half-gathered paste, which was not a coverage gap but dead code, measured
// unreachable with instrumentation and removed.
//
// A program with the shape of the one interface built on this — level-triggered
// epoll on fd 0, one read_key per readiness report — driven through a real pty
// with: a bracketed paste of two hundred characters (201 keystrokes out, and 0
// before the paste was fixed); `ESC ESC x y` (27, 120, 121, against 27, 27, 121
// before); Escape then a bracketed paste (27 and then the text, no bracket bytes
// typed); an unsolicited OSC 11 reply, a DCS reply, an OSC 52 clipboard reply,
// SS2, APC, PM, and a negative-coordinate mouse report — zero keystrokes each,
// against 23, 6, 15, 1, 1 and 5 before; arrows, SS3 arrows and a bare Escape
// unchanged.
//
// An event-driven program on a 24x80 pty, for the mouse gate: `<0;40;12M`
// delivered, and `<0;200;12M`, `<0;40;999M` and a twenty-digit column dropped,
// where all four were delivered before.
//
// The same shape with the reader OPEN — input_open(shared_input(), 0), which
// turns focus reporting on for every caller — fed twenty-two focus reports and
// then `hello` and a `Q`: six keystrokes out and the loop ends, against NOTHING
// out and a loop that never ended, because the burst crossed read_key's byte
// ceiling inside one call and the skip that followed ate the typing. Again with
// mouse reporting on and five SGR reports in front of the focus ones: the same
// six, against the same nothing.
//
// One byte per read(2), and one sequence per call, measured under strace with
// the reader's modes ON — the configuration where this was false: three SGR
// reports and a keystroke cost 11, 11, 11 and 1 reads across four calls and
// answer KEY_NONE, KEY_NONE, KEY_NONE, 'x', where they used to cost 34 reads in
// the first call and answer 'x' from it.
//
// Where the bytes wait, measured with FIONREAD: five
// typed bytes drain the kernel queue 5,4,3,2,1 with this module's buffer at 0; a
// mouse report leaves exactly the byte behind it in the kernel; a paste hands its
// bytes out one per call with the rest still in the KERNEL queue, where a poller
// can see them. Late opening of fd 0, under strace: a program that imports this
// module for set_color() and clear_screen() makes zero read syscalls.
//
// 200000 random hostile buffers through _scan with a canary after the Event
// struct, and 600 more through the reader over pipes: no hang, no over-read, no
// coordinate on an event that is not EV_MOUSE, no coordinate outside its bounds,
// no key in [32,1000) that was not a literal byte of the input. Paste assembly
// compared byte for byte at 200, 4096, 8192, 8193, 8200, 16384, 16387 and 30000
// bytes: 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3 and 4 events, every byte accounted for.
//
// eutimico rebuilt against this file (a copy of its tree pointed at this std):
// clean build. It uses enter_raw, leave_raw, get_size, read_key and the KEY_*
// constants and nothing else, and those constants are byte-identical to what
// they were before the reader was rewritten — KEY_NUL is an addition, and
// nothing was renumbered to fit it. That interface compares against the names in
// twenty-four places and also tests `k >= 32 && k < 1000` for "a byte a person
// typed"; 1009 is outside that window, so Ctrl+Space now arrives as a key it
// ignores instead of as a key it cannot see. Its pty output is NOT byte-identical
// to the old reader's any more, and cannot be: the point of the change is that an
// OSC reply no longer becomes keystrokes and `ESC ESC x` no longer loses the x.
//
// All of that was Linux on x86_64. The Windows and AArch64 targets are known to
// COMPILE and nothing more.

use "os.cst" as os;
if (__target_is_linux + __compat_mode)   use "os/linux.cst"   as linux;
if (__target_is_windows + __compat_mode) use "os/windows.cst" as win;
use "io.cst" as io;
use "mem.cst" as mem;

// Cached pre-raw console modes on Windows so leave_raw can restore them.
let is u32 as _win_orig_in_mode  with mut = 0;
let is u32 as _win_orig_out_mode with mut = 0;

// --- termios ---
// Linux struct termios: 60 bytes
//   c_iflag (4) + c_oflag (4) + c_cflag (4) + c_lflag (4)
//   + c_line (1) + c_cc[32] (32) + pad(3) + c_ispeed (4) + c_ospeed (4)
// Total: 56 bytes (packed: 4+4+4+4+1+32+3+4+4 = 60, but kernel uses 60)
// We store as raw 60-byte buffer.

let is i64 as TERMIOS_SIZE with imut = 60;

// ioctl requests
let is i64 as TCGETS  with imut = 21505;  // 0x5401
let is i64 as TCSETS  with imut = 21506;  // 0x5402
let is i64 as TIOCGWINSZ with imut = 21523; // 0x5413

// c_lflag bits to clear for raw mode
let is i32 as ECHO   with imut = 8;     // 0x0008
let is i32 as ICANON with imut = 2;     // 0x0002
let is i32 as ISIG   with imut = 1;     // 0x0001
let is i32 as IEXTEN with imut = 32768; // 0x8000

// c_iflag bits to clear
let is i32 as ICRNL  with imut = 256;   // 0x0100
let is i32 as IXON   with imut = 1024;  // 0x0400

// Saved original termios
let is [60]u8 as _orig_termios with mut;
let is i32 as _raw_active with mut = 0;

fn enter_raw() as void {
    if (_raw_active == 1) { return; }
    if (os.current() == os.OS_LINUX) {
        // Save current
        linux.ioctl(linux.STDIN, TCGETS, cast(*u8, &_orig_termios));
        // Copy and modify
        let is [60]u8 as raw;
        mem.memcpy(cast(*u8, &raw), cast(*u8, &_orig_termios), 60);
        // c_iflag at offset 0: clear ICRNL | IXON
        let is *i32 as iflag = cast(*i32, &raw);
        *iflag = *iflag & (cast(i32, 0 - 1) - ICRNL - IXON);
        // c_lflag at offset 12: clear ECHO | ICANON | ISIG | IEXTEN
        let is *i32 as lflag = cast(*i32, cast(i64, &raw) + 12);
        *lflag = *lflag & (cast(i32, 0 - 1) - ECHO - ICANON - ISIG - IEXTEN);
        // c_cc starts at offset 17 — set VMIN=0, VTIME=1 (100ms timeout).
        let is *u8 as cc = cast(*u8, cast(i64, &raw) + 17);
        cc[6] = 0;
        cc[5] = 1;
        linux.ioctl(linux.STDIN, TCSETS, cast(*u8, &raw));
        _raw_active = 1;
    } else if (os.current() == os.OS_WINDOWS) {
        let is i64 as hin  = win.GetStdHandle(win.STD_INPUT_HANDLE);
        let is i64 as hout = win.GetStdHandle(win.STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE);
        win.GetConsoleMode(hin, &_win_orig_in_mode);
        win.GetConsoleMode(hout, &_win_orig_out_mode);
        // Clear LINE_INPUT|ECHO_INPUT|PROCESSED_INPUT (1|2|4 = 7), enable VT input.
        let is u32 as raw_in = (_win_orig_in_mode & cast(u32, 0 - 8)) | win.ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_INPUT;
        win.SetConsoleMode(hin, raw_in);
        // Enable VT processing for ANSI escapes on output.
        let is u32 as raw_out = _win_orig_out_mode | win.ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_PROCESSING;
        win.SetConsoleMode(hout, raw_out);
        _raw_active = 1;
    }
}

fn leave_raw() as void {
    if (_raw_active == 0) { return; }
    if (os.current() == os.OS_LINUX) {
        linux.ioctl(linux.STDIN, TCSETS, cast(*u8, &_orig_termios));
    } else if (os.current() == os.OS_WINDOWS) {
        let is i64 as hin  = win.GetStdHandle(win.STD_INPUT_HANDLE);
        let is i64 as hout = win.GetStdHandle(win.STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE);
        win.SetConsoleMode(hin,  _win_orig_in_mode);
        win.SetConsoleMode(hout, _win_orig_out_mode);
    }
    _raw_active = 0;
}

// --- Terminal size ---

fn get_size(rows as *i32, cols as *i32) as void {
    if (os.current() == os.OS_LINUX) {
        // struct winsize: rows(u16) cols(u16) xpixel(u16) ypixel(u16) = 8 bytes
        let is [8]u8 as ws;
        linux.ioctl(linux.STDIN, TIOCGWINSZ, cast(*u8, &ws));
        let is *u16 as vals = cast(*u16, &ws);
        *rows = cast(i32, vals[0]);
        *cols = cast(i32, vals[1]);
    } else if (os.current() == os.OS_WINDOWS) {
        // CONSOLE_SCREEN_BUFFER_INFO is 22 bytes; srWindow (4 i16) at offset 10.
        let is [22]u8 as info;
        let is i64 as h = win.GetStdHandle(win.STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE);
        if (win.GetConsoleScreenBufferInfo(h, cast(*u8, &info)) == 0) {
            *rows = 24; *cols = 80;
            return;
        }
        let is *i16 as v = cast(*i16, cast(i64, &info) + 10);
        let is i16 as left   = v[0];
        let is i16 as top    = v[1];
        let is i16 as right  = v[2];
        let is i16 as bottom = v[3];
        *cols = cast(i32, right) - cast(i32, left) + 1;
        *rows = cast(i32, bottom) - cast(i32, top) + 1;
    }
}

// --- ANSI output ---

fn esc_byte() as void {
    let is u8 as e = 27;
    io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, &e, 1);
}

fn clear_screen() as void {
    io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[2J\x1b[H", 10);
}

fn move_to(row as i32, col as i32) as void {
    let is [32]u8 as buf;
    let is i64 as n = io.sprintf(cast(*u8, &buf), 32, "\x1b[%d;%dH", cast(i64, row), cast(i64, col));
    io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, cast(*u8, &buf), n);
}

fn hide_cursor() as void {
    io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[?25l", 6);
}

fn show_cursor() as void {
    io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[?25h", 6);
}

fn clear_line() as void {
    io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[2K", 4);
}

fn set_color(fg as i32) as void {
    let is [16]u8 as buf;
    let is i64 as n = io.sprintf(cast(*u8, &buf), 16, "\x1b[%dm", cast(i64, fg));
    io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, cast(*u8, &buf), n);
}

fn set_bold() as void {
    io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[1m", 4);
}

fn reset_style() as void {
    io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[0m", 4);
}

// --- Color constants ---
let is i32 as BLACK   with imut = 30;
let is i32 as RED     with imut = 31;
let is i32 as GREEN   with imut = 32;
let is i32 as YELLOW  with imut = 33;
let is i32 as BLUE    with imut = 34;
let is i32 as MAGENTA with imut = 35;
let is i32 as CYAN    with imut = 36;
let is i32 as WHITE   with imut = 37;

// --- Key constants ---
let is i32 as KEY_NONE  with imut = 0;
let is i32 as KEY_UP    with imut = 1000;
let is i32 as KEY_DOWN  with imut = 1001;
let is i32 as KEY_RIGHT with imut = 1002;
let is i32 as KEY_LEFT  with imut = 1003;
let is i32 as KEY_HOME  with imut = 1004;
let is i32 as KEY_END   with imut = 1005;
let is i32 as KEY_DEL   with imut = 1006;
let is i32 as KEY_PGUP  with imut = 1007;
let is i32 as KEY_PGDN  with imut = 1008;
let is i32 as KEY_ESC   with imut = 27;
let is i32 as KEY_ENTER with imut = 13;
let is i32 as KEY_TAB   with imut = 9;
let is i32 as KEY_BKSP  with imut = 127;

// The NUL byte — Ctrl+@ and Ctrl+Space — as read_key() answers it, and only
// there. It is not a code a terminal sends and not a code the parser produces:
// input_next reports that byte as EV_KEY with key=0, because a kind and a key
// together CAN say "a key whose value is zero" and a single i32 cannot.
// read_key's 0 is KEY_NONE, "nothing to report" — a normal answer under VMIN=0,
// and the one answer every caller guards on — so a NUL handed back as 0 is a
// keystroke that every caller drops.
//
// 1009 is the next number after the cursor-key block above, and it is a choice
// bounded by two things this module does not get to break. It collides with
// nothing read_key can already answer: a byte 0..255, one of KEY_UP..KEY_PGDN
// (1000..1008), KEY_ESC, KEY_ENTER, KEY_TAB, KEY_BKSP. And it sits outside
// [32,1000), the window a consumer uses to mean "a byte a person typed" — a NUL
// is not that, and must not end up inserted into a text buffer by a caller that
// tests the range instead of the names. No existing constant moved to make room
// for it: consumers were compiled against these numbers.
let is i32 as KEY_NUL   with imut = 1009;

// =======================================================
// Event-based input
// =======================================================

let is i64 as EV_NONE  with imut = 0;
let is i64 as EV_KEY   with imut = 1;
let is i64 as EV_MOUSE with imut = 2;
let is i64 as EV_PASTE with imut = 3;
// 4 is EV_RESIZE in the design, and is left unused on purpose so that a later
// commit can add it without renumbering. Why it is not here: see the header.
let is i64 as EV_FOCUS with imut = 5;

// Internal kinds. The paste brackets are sequences, not events: _scan reports
// them, input_next acts on them, and neither ever reaches a caller.
let is i64 as _EV_PASTE_ON  with imut = 100;
let is i64 as _EV_PASTE_OFF with imut = 101;

// Event.button carries the SGR button field with the modifier and the motion
// bit removed, so these constants ARE the wire numbers — which is why a button
// this list does not name (8..11 on a five-button mouse) still arrives intact
// instead of being flattened into one of the three below.
let is i64 as MB_LEFT        with imut = 0;
let is i64 as MB_MIDDLE      with imut = 1;
let is i64 as MB_RIGHT       with imut = 2;
let is i64 as MB_NONE        with imut = 3;    // motion with nothing held down
let is i64 as MB_WHEEL_UP    with imut = 64;
let is i64 as MB_WHEEL_DOWN  with imut = 65;
let is i64 as MB_WHEEL_LEFT  with imut = 66;
let is i64 as MB_WHEEL_RIGHT with imut = 67;

// Event.down. EV_FOCUS reuses the field with 1 = gained, 0 = lost.
let is i32 as EV_RELEASE with imut = 0;
let is i32 as EV_PRESS   with imut = 1;
let is i32 as EV_MOTION  with imut = 2;

// Event.mods — xterm's modifier bits, shifted down to bit 0. A mouse report
// carries them in bits 2..4 of the button byte; a cursor key carries them as
// `param - 1` in its second parameter. Both land here.
let is i64 as MOD_SHIFT with imut = 1;
let is i64 as MOD_ALT   with imut = 2;
let is i64 as MOD_CTRL  with imut = 4;

struct Event {
    kind   as i64;
    key    as i32;    // EV_KEY: the codes read_key returns, with ONE difference —
                      // the NUL byte is 0 here and KEY_NUL there. See KEY_NUL.
    button as i64;    // EV_MOUSE: MB_*
    down   as i32;    // EV_PRESS / EV_RELEASE / EV_MOTION
    row    as i64;    // EV_MOUSE: 1-based, as the terminal reports it
    col    as i64;
    mods   as i64;    // MOD_* bits
    text   as *u8;    // EV_PASTE: owned by the reader. See below for how long
    len    as i64;    //           it stays valid, which is not long.
}

// Event.text points into the reader's own paste buffer, so it is valid until the
// next call that writes there, and exactly one entry point does: input_next, and
// only while it is gathering a paste. read_key never writes into it — it cannot
// answer with a paste — though it does END a gathering input_next had begun, and
// the bytes gathered by then are already out by that point. A caller that needs
// the bytes after that copies them. The chunks of a paste larger than IN_PASTE all arrive at the
// SAME address, one after another, so comparing pointers is not a way to tell
// one chunk from the next; ev.len is.

// 4096 is the size of the kernel's tty line-discipline buffer on Linux
// (N_TTY_BUF_SIZE), so one read of this size empties a queue that is as full
// as the kernel will ever let it get, and a burst never needs two syscalls.
let is i64 as IN_BUF with imut = 4096;

// Chosen, not derived: a paste longer than this arrives as more than one
// EV_PASTE, split at the cap, with no byte lost. The design's worked example is
// two hundred characters; this is forty times that.
//
// What it costs is the whole struct, not this array: the module keeps one Input
// in .bss for read_key to read through, and sizeof(Input) is 12348 bytes —
// measured, this array plus IN_BUF plus the bookkeeping. A program that imports
// this module only for set_color() and clear_screen() measures 12512 bytes of
// .bss in total, so this is essentially all of it.
let is i64 as IN_PASTE with imut = 8192;

// How many bytes read_key() will pull while assembling ONE keystroke before it
// stops and skips the rest of the sequence. The longest sequence a terminal
// sends as a KEY is an SGR mouse report at four-digit coordinates — 17 bytes —
// so this is nearly four times the worst legitimate case for a keystroke. It
// exists to keep one call costing about one keystroke; in normal use read_key
// reads one byte, or three for an arrow key. A longer sequence is not dropped
// when this is reached: it is skipped to its terminator, so no part of it comes
// back as text. A reply longer than this (an OSC 52 clipboard, say) is therefore
// read as nothing by read_key, and as nothing by input_next too.
//
// "Skipped to its terminator" needs bytes that say WHICH terminator, which is
// the first two of the sequence. Below two, there is no sequence in the buffer to
// skip and _skip_begin says so by doing nothing: the bytes stay and the next call
// reads on. A state that only arose from a counter that had stopped meaning one
// sequence's bytes — see read_key — and the guard is kept anyway, because the
// default it used to fall through to discards a person's typing.
let is i64 as _RK_MAX with imut = 64;

// An Input must start zeroed, which a global in .bss is and a stack local is
// not. `mark` is what says which of three states it is in, and it holds a 64-bit
// constant rather than a 1 for a reason: the question "has this been
// initialised?" is being asked of memory that may be an old stack frame, and a
// frame matching a one-bit answer is likely while one matching this is not.
//
//   0, or anything else   never initialised — the input calls reset it
//   _MARK_READY           initialised, no terminal mode set through it
//   _MARK_OPEN            between input_open and input_close, so m_* below is
//                         an undo record and NOT to be zeroed
//
// input_open initialises unconditionally, including on the path that refuses.
// A caller that skips input_open — read-only use of input_next on a descriptor
// it set itself — either uses a global or zeroes the struct.
let is i64 as _MARK_READY with imut = 0x7465726D5F726479;   // "term_rdy"
let is i64 as _MARK_OPEN  with imut = 0x7465726D5F6F706E;   // "term_opn"

struct Input {
    fd      as i64;
    mark    as i64;     // which of the three states above
    m_mouse as i32;     // which modes are actually on, so input_close can
    m_paste as i32;     // undo exactly those and nothing else
    m_focus as i32;
    head    as i64;     // first byte not yet parsed
    tail    as i64;     // one past the last byte read
    plen    as i64;     // bytes accumulated for the EV_PASTE being assembled
    inpaste as i32;
    skip    as i32;     // 0, or which terminator the reader is discarding
                        // bytes until: 1 = a CSI final byte, 2 = ST or BEL
    buf     as [IN_BUF]u8;
    text    as [IN_PASTE]u8;
}

// The reader read_key() uses, and the one a program that wants both APIs against
// one reader passes to input_open — see shared_input() below, which is how it is
// reached. Initialised on the first input call and never before: a program that
// imports this module for set_color() must not find fd 0 taken from under it, and
// examples/calculator.cst is exactly that program — it reads fd 0 itself, one
// byte at a time, in the same file that calls clear_screen().
//
// It is a whole Input, so it costs sizeof(Input) of .bss in any program that
// imports this module, whether or not that program ever reads a key. See the
// IN_PASTE note above for the measured number.
let is Input as _shell with mut;

fn _ev_clear(ev as *Event) as void {
    ev.kind = EV_NONE;
    ev.key = KEY_NONE;
    ev.button = 0;
    ev.down = 0;
    ev.row = 0;
    ev.col = 0;
    ev.mods = 0;
    ev.text = cast(*u8, 0);
    ev.len = 0;
}

// The most digits a parameter may carry. Nine is where the accumulator below
// cannot overflow (999999999 * 10 + 9 is nowhere near i64's ceiling) and it is
// four more than any sequence this parser reads actually uses — a four-digit
// coordinate, a three-digit tilde code, one digit of modifier. Ten digits is
// therefore not a parameter, and saying so is how a number that does not fit is
// REFUSED instead of being invented.
let is i64 as _NUM_DIGITS with imut = 9;

// The widest coordinate that can belong to a terminal. Derived, not chosen: a
// Linux winsize carries rows and columns as u16 and Windows reports them as i16,
// so a coordinate past 65535 is not describing any terminal whose size this
// module can ask for. It is a sanity bound on the parser, which is pure and
// cannot ask; the reader checks against the descriptor's ACTUAL size on top of
// it — see _mouse_fits.
let is i64 as _COORD_MAX with imut = 65535;

// The widest SGR button field. Its bits are button (0..2), shift (4), meta (8),
// ctrl (16), motion (32), wheel (64) and the fourth/fifth button (128); nothing
// above 255 has a meaning, so a larger number is not a button.
let is i64 as _BUTTON_MAX with imut = 255;

// Read a decimal number starting at buf[*i], advancing *i past it.
//
// Two things can go wrong here and they are NOT the same answer, so they do not
// share a channel. The RETURN is -1 for both — "no number for you" — and *over,
// written on every call, says which: 0 when there was no digit at buf[*i], which
// means the parameter is absent and is a legal shape for several sequences here,
// and 1 when there were digits and more than _NUM_DIGITS of them, which is no
// shape at all. Twenty digits of column used to arrive as
// col=7766279631452241919: positive, past every guard, delivered as a report.
//
// A caller that refuses on `< 0` refuses both, and that is right for every
// number a report is built out of. A caller that has a DEFAULT for "absent" must
// look at *over before applying it, because a default is an answer and a number
// that does not fit has none: `ESC [ 1 ; 9999999999 A` used to arrive as an
// arrow key with NO modifier, because _csi_mods tested `m < 1` — the same test
// it uses for "there is no second parameter" — and answered with the default 0.
// One number that does not fit, refused in a mouse report and invented in a
// modifier, out of one -1 meaning two things.
//
// That is why the flag is an argument and not a convention: a new call site
// cannot be written without naming a place to put it.
fn _num(buf as *u8, n as i64, i as *i64, over as *i32) as i64 {
    *over = 0;
    let is i64 as p with mut = *i;
    if (p >= n) { return 0 - 1; }
    let is i64 as c = cast(i64, buf[p]);
    if (c < 48 || c > 57) { return 0 - 1; }
    let is i64 as v with mut = 0;
    let is i64 as digits with mut = 0;
    while (p < n) {
        let is i64 as d = cast(i64, buf[p]);
        if (d < 48 || d > 57) { break; }
        digits = digits + 1;
        if (digits > _NUM_DIGITS) { *over = 1; return 0 - 1; }
        v = v * 10 + (d - 48);
        p = p + 1;
    }
    *i = p;
    return v;
}

// Does an ESC followed by this byte begin something, rather than being a key?
//
// CSI, SS3, SS2 and the five string introducers — OSC, DCS, SOS, PM, APC — plus
// ESC itself, which begins the same decision one byte later. It exists for the
// ESC ESC rule: the difference between an ESC that must be left for the next
// scan and one that must be consumed now is exactly this list.
fn _introduces(b as i64) as i32 {
    if (b == 91) { return 1; }         // '[' CSI
    if (b == 79) { return 1; }         // 'O' SS3
    if (b == 78) { return 1; }         // 'N' SS2
    if (b == 93) { return 1; }         // ']' OSC
    if (b == 80) { return 1; }         // 'P' DCS
    if (b == 88) { return 1; }         // 'X' SOS
    if (b == 94) { return 1; }         // '^' PM
    if (b == 95) { return 1; }         // '_' APC
    if (b == 27) { return 1; }         // ESC, deciding again one byte along
    return 0;
}

// The final byte of a cursor-key sequence, for both CSI and SS3 forms.
fn _final_key(f as i64) as i32 {
    if (f == 65) { return KEY_UP; }
    if (f == 66) { return KEY_DOWN; }
    if (f == 67) { return KEY_RIGHT; }
    if (f == 68) { return KEY_LEFT; }
    if (f == 72) { return KEY_HOME; }
    if (f == 70) { return KEY_END; }
    return KEY_NONE;
}

// xterm sends `ESC [ 1 ; 5 A` for Ctrl+Up. `p` points at whatever follows the
// first parameter; a second parameter, minus one, is the modifier bitmask.
//
// No second parameter means no modifiers, and the 0 returned here is that
// answer. A second parameter that does not FIT is a different thing and does not
// get that answer: it makes the sequence unreadable, and it leaves through
// *over, which the caller turns into "consumed whole, reported as nothing". The
// `m < 1` below used to be reached by both, so a refused number came out as a
// key with no modifier.
fn _csi_mods(buf as *u8, n as i64, p as i64, over as *i32) as i64 {
    *over = 0;
    if (p >= n) { return 0; }
    if (cast(i64, buf[p]) != 59) { return 0; }        // ';'
    let is i64 as q with mut = p + 1;
    let is i64 as m = _num(buf, n, &q, over);
    if (m < 1) { return 0; }
    return (m - 1) & 7;
}

// SGR 1006: ESC [ < Cb ; Cx ; Cy M|m. `buf`/`n` cover the parameter bytes only
// (everything between the '<' and the final byte).
//
// Every exit before the last one leaves the event as EV_NONE with row and col
// still zero. That is the point: a report we cannot read all three numbers out
// of must not produce a coordinate, because a coordinate is something a caller
// will click on.
fn _mouse(buf as *u8, n as i64, fin as i64, ev as *Event) as void {
    let is i64 as i with mut = 0;
    // Both of _num's failures are one answer here — a report with a number
    // missing and a report with a number that does not fit are equally not a
    // report — so every number below is refused on the return and the flag is
    // never read. It is passed anyway, because there is nowhere else to put it.
    let is i32 as ov with mut = 0;
    let is i64 as cb = _num(buf, n, &i, &ov);
    if (cb < 0) { return; }
    if (i >= n) { return; }
    if (cast(i64, buf[i]) != 59) { return; }
    i = i + 1;
    let is i64 as cx = _num(buf, n, &i, &ov);
    if (cx < 0) { return; }
    if (i >= n) { return; }
    if (cast(i64, buf[i]) != 59) { return; }
    i = i + 1;
    let is i64 as cy = _num(buf, n, &i, &ov);
    if (cy < 0) { return; }
    if (i != n) { return; }              // trailing parameters: not this shape
    // Every number is in range or there is no report. SGR counts cells from one,
    // so zero is not a coordinate either, and a button field is eight bits.
    if (cb > _BUTTON_MAX) { return; }
    if (cx < 1 || cx > _COORD_MAX) { return; }
    if (cy < 1 || cy > _COORD_MAX) { return; }
    ev.kind = EV_MOUSE;
    ev.button = (cb & 3) + (cb & 192);
    ev.mods = (cb / 4) & 7;
    ev.col = cx;
    ev.row = cy;
    if (fin == 109) { ev.down = EV_RELEASE; }         // 'm'
    else if ((cb & 32) != 0) { ev.down = EV_MOTION; }
    else { ev.down = EV_PRESS; }
}

// Scan one event out of buf[0..n).
//
// Returns the number of bytes it consumed — at least 1 — or 0 to say "these
// bytes are the start of something longer, feed me more". `last` = 1 means no
// more bytes are coming, so a prefix has to resolve now.
//
// Pure: no descriptor, no global, no allocation. That is what makes the mouse,
// the paste brackets and every truncation case testable with no terminal in
// reach, which is the only way any of this was checked at all.
fn _scan(buf as *u8, n as i64, last as i32, ev as *Event) as i64 {
    _ev_clear(ev);
    if (n <= 0) { return 0; }
    let is i64 as b0 = cast(i64, buf[0]);
    if (b0 != 27) {
        // Every non-ESC byte is its own event, in order, including UTF-8
        // continuation bytes (128..255) — a caller assembling characters needs
        // them one at a time and unmolested.
        ev.kind = EV_KEY;
        ev.key = cast(i32, b0);
        return 1;
    }
    // ESC with nothing behind it is the Escape key, and only `last` can say
    // that nothing is behind it. In raw mode that answer costs VTIME=1, one
    // read that times out at 100 ms, and it is the most-used key in the only
    // interface built on this module — so it is deliberately paid, not avoided.
    if (n == 1) {
        if (last == 0) { return 0; }
        ev.kind = EV_KEY;
        ev.key = KEY_ESC;
        return 1;
    }
    let is i64 as b1 = cast(i64, buf[1]);
    if (b1 == 27) {
        // ESC then ESC, and the byte after the second one decides which of them
        // is consumed. Both readings are wrong on their own:
        //
        //   - consume only the first, always: the ESC left behind meets the
        //     ESC+byte rule below on the next scan, which answers Escape having
        //     eaten the byte. `ESC ESC x` then loses the x — measured in a pty,
        //     27, 27 and no 120 at all.
        //   - consume both, always: a second ESC that was introducing something
        //     is gone, and its tail is typed as text. Found by driving a pty
        //     with Escape followed by a bracketed paste — the paste's own ESC
        //     was eaten and `[200~` went into the input.
        //
        // So: if the second ESC introduces a sequence, consume only the first
        // and let the next scan parse that sequence; otherwise the two ESCs are
        // one Escape and the byte behind them is untouched. Nothing is guessed —
        // when the deciding byte is not here yet, this asks for it.
        if (n == 2) {
            if (last == 0) { return 0; }
            ev.kind = EV_KEY;
            ev.key = KEY_ESC;
            return 2;                  // two ESCs, nothing behind: one Escape
        }
        ev.kind = EV_KEY;
        ev.key = KEY_ESC;
        if (_introduces(cast(i64, buf[2])) == 1) { return 1; }
        return 2;
    }
    if (b1 == 79) {                                   // 'O' — SS3
        if (n < 3) {
            if (last == 0) { return 0; }
            ev.kind = EV_KEY;
            ev.key = KEY_ESC;
            return 2;
        }
        let is i32 as k = _final_key(cast(i64, buf[2]));
        if (k != KEY_NONE) { ev.kind = EV_KEY; ev.key = k; }
        return 3;
    }
    if (b1 == 78) {                                   // 'N' — SS2
        // One byte of payload and no key of ours behind it: consumed whole and
        // reported as nothing, exactly like the SS3 form above.
        if (n < 3) {
            if (last == 0) { return 0; }
            // Two bytes and no payload coming. This is what Alt+N arrives as,
            // and no Escape is invented for it — see the string case below.
            return 2;
        }
        return 3;
    }
    if (b1 == 93 || b1 == 80 || b1 == 88 || b1 == 94 || b1 == 95) {
        // The string-type sequences: OSC (ESC ]), DCS (ESC P), SOS (ESC X),
        // PM (ESC ^), APC (ESC _). Their payload is arbitrary text and the
        // terminator is ST (ESC \) or, for OSC, BEL.
        //
        // This is the family the module exists to swallow, and the one where
        // "an unknown sequence leaves nothing behind" was false: a 25-byte
        // OSC 11 background-colour reply — which some terminals send unasked —
        // arrived as an Escape plus its own text, 23 keystrokes, the first of
        // them the one that opens a menu in the interface built on this.
        let is i64 as i with mut = 2;
        while (i < n) {
            let is i64 as c = cast(i64, buf[i]);
            if (c == 7) { return i + 1; }                    // BEL ends OSC
            if (c == 27) {
                if (i + 1 >= n) {
                    if (last == 0) { return 0; }             // an ST, split
                    return i;                                // abandoned here
                }
                if (cast(i64, buf[i + 1]) == 92) { return i + 2; }   // ESC \ = ST
                // An ESC that is not the start of an ST ends the string and
                // belongs to whatever comes next — often a real arrow key.
                return i;
            }
            if (c < 32) {
                // A C0 control is not payload. It is a keystroke that arrived
                // while the string was still open — Enter, most often — so the
                // string ends here and the byte is left for the next scan.
                return i;
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if (last == 0) { return 0; }
        // No terminator, and nothing more is coming. The two readings are not
        // symmetric in what they destroy: swallowing everything eats what a
        // person typed after an Alt+] or Alt+P, which arrive as exactly these
        // two bytes and are never followed by a terminator, while consuming only
        // the introducer risks typing the tail of a reply that a terminal split
        // across the 100 ms window — which a terminal, writing its reply in one
        // write, does not do. Losing what a person typed is the worse of the
        // two. So the introducer goes and nothing is reported: answering Escape
        // here is what turned unsolicited replies into menu toggles.
        return 2;
    }
    if (b1 != 91) {                                   // not '[': no sequence
        // Answering Escape here and eating the byte is what read_key has
        // always done, and it is kept: the alternative reading is Alt+key,
        // this module does not model Alt, and a mods bit invented here would
        // change the value read_key returns for a key that consumers already
        // compare against KEY_*.
        ev.kind = EV_KEY;
        ev.key = KEY_ESC;
        return 2;
    }
    // CSI. Parameter bytes are 0x30..0x3F, intermediates 0x20..0x2F, and one
    // final byte 0x40..0x7E closes it. Walking to that final byte — instead of
    // guessing from the third byte — is the whole of the fix: the sequence is
    // consumed as a unit whether or not anything below recognises it.
    //
    // Both ranges are walked by ONE loop, in any order. Requiring parameters
    // before intermediates is what a terminal sends, but treating a violation
    // as the end of the sequence handed the rest of it back as text: a minus
    // sign is 0x2D, an intermediate, so `ESC [ < 0 ; - 1 ; 1 2 M` stopped at the
    // digit after it, consumed six bytes, and typed `1;12M` into the input.
    let is i64 as i with mut = 2;
    while (i < n) {
        let is i64 as c = cast(i64, buf[i]);
        if (c < 32 || c > 63) { break; }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    if (i >= n) {
        if (last == 0) { return 0; }
        // Truncated and nothing more is coming. `ESC [` alone is what the old
        // reader answered KEY_ESC for, so it still does; anything longer had
        // begun to carry parameters, and manufacturing a keystroke out of half
        // a report is the defect this rewrite exists to remove.
        if (n == 2) { ev.kind = EV_KEY; ev.key = KEY_ESC; }
        return n;
    }
    let is i64 as f = cast(i64, buf[i]);
    if (f < 64 || f > 126) {
        // What stopped the loop is neither a parameter, an intermediate, nor a
        // final byte: a C0 control, DEL, or a byte with the high bit set. None
        // of those can appear inside a CSI, and all of them can be typed, so the
        // sequence ends here and the byte is left alone — it is a real
        // keystroke, and this is the one case where handing a byte back is the
        // honest answer. `ESC [ 1` then Enter is the shape.
        return i;
    }
    let is i64 as total = i + 1;
    if (cast(i64, buf[2]) == 60 && (f == 77 || f == 109)) {      // '<', 'M'/'m'
        _mouse(cast(*u8, cast(i64, buf) + 3), i - 3, f, ev);
        return total;
    }
    if (f == 126) {                                              // '~'
        let is i64 as p with mut = 2;
        let is i32 as ov with mut = 0;
        // A leading parameter that does not fit needs no check of its own here:
        // -1 is none of the codes below, so such a sequence already falls
        // through to "consumed whole, reported as nothing".
        let is i64 as v = _num(buf, i, &p, &ov);
        if (v == 1 || v == 7) { ev.kind = EV_KEY; ev.key = KEY_HOME; }
        else if (v == 3) { ev.kind = EV_KEY; ev.key = KEY_DEL; }
        else if (v == 4 || v == 8) { ev.kind = EV_KEY; ev.key = KEY_END; }
        else if (v == 5) { ev.kind = EV_KEY; ev.key = KEY_PGUP; }
        else if (v == 6) { ev.kind = EV_KEY; ev.key = KEY_PGDN; }
        else if (v == 200) { ev.kind = _EV_PASTE_ON; }
        else if (v == 201) { ev.kind = _EV_PASTE_OFF; }
        if (ev.kind == EV_KEY) {
            ev.mods = _csi_mods(buf, i, p, &ov);
            // A modifier that does not fit unmakes the key: Delete with a
            // parameter this parser refused is not Delete, and answering it with
            // "no modifier" is inventing the half of the sequence that was
            // refused.
            if (ov == 1) { _ev_clear(ev); }
        }
        return total;
    }
    let is i32 as k = _final_key(f);
    if (k != KEY_NONE) {
        let is i64 as p with mut = 2;
        let is i32 as ov with mut = 0;
        _num(buf, i, &p, &ov);                // the leading 1, when it is sent
        // The leading parameter's VALUE is discarded — the call is here to step
        // over it — but "it did not fit" is not a value, and it is exactly what
        // separates `ESC [ A`, an arrow with no parameter, from
        // `ESC [ 9999999999 A`, an arrow behind a number this parser refused.
        // The second one is consumed whole and reported as nothing.
        if (ov == 1) { return total; }
        ev.kind = EV_KEY;
        ev.key = k;
        ev.mods = _csi_mods(buf, i, p, &ov);
        if (ov == 1) { _ev_clear(ev); }
        return total;
    }
    if (f == 73) { ev.kind = EV_FOCUS; ev.down = 1; return total; }   // 'I'
    if (f == 79) { ev.kind = EV_FOCUS; ev.down = 0; return total; }   // 'O'
    // Understood as a sequence, not understood as an event: consumed whole and
    // reported as nothing. Reporting Escape instead is what turned a terminal's
    // unsolicited replies into keystrokes.
    return total;
}

// Everything about the reading, and nothing about the terminal. A second
// input_open on the same Input goes through here and not through _reset: the
// mode flags are the undo record input_close is gated on, and zeroing them is
// how mouse reporting stayed on in the terminal after close.
fn _reset_buf(in as *Input) as void {
    in.fd = io.STDIN;
    in.head = 0;
    in.tail = 0;
    in.plen = 0;
    in.inpaste = 0;
    in.skip = 0;
}

// The whole struct, for an Input that has never been opened. Only correct when
// nothing has been set through it, which is what the mark is there to tell.
fn _reset(in as *Input) as void {
    _reset_buf(in);
    in.mark = _MARK_READY;
    in.m_mouse = 0;
    in.m_paste = 0;
    in.m_focus = 0;
}

// Has this Input been through _reset? Asked of memory that may be a dead frame.
fn _live(in as *Input) as i32 {
    if (in.mark == _MARK_READY) { return 1; }
    if (in.mark == _MARK_OPEN) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

// Is there anything to read right now, without waiting for it?
fn _pending(fd as i64) as i32 {
    if (os.current() == os.OS_LINUX) {
        // struct pollfd { int fd; short events; short revents; } — 8 bytes.
        let is [8]u8 as pfd;
        let is *i32 as pf = cast(*i32, &pfd);
        *pf = cast(i32, fd);
        let is *i16 as pe = cast(*i16, cast(i64, &pfd) + 4);
        *pe = cast(i16, linux.POLLIN);
        let is *i16 as pr = cast(*i16, cast(i64, &pfd) + 6);
        *pr = 0;
        if (linux.poll(cast(*u8, &pfd), 1, 0) <= 0) { return 0; }
        return 1;
    } else if (os.current() == os.OS_WINDOWS) {
        // UNVERIFIED — see the module header. A console handle signals when an
        // input RECORD is available, which is not the same claim as "ReadFile
        // will return bytes".
        let is i64 as hin = win.GetStdHandle(win.STD_INPUT_HANDLE);
        if (win.WaitForSingleObject(hin, 0) == cast(u32, 0)) { return 1; }
        return 0;
    }
    return 0;
}

// Get more bytes. `want` caps the read; `force` = 1 reads without asking
// whether anything is pending, which is how the 100 ms that resolves a bare
// Escape gets paid, and how read_key keeps its old one-byte-per-read shape.
fn _fill(in as *Input, want as i64, force as i32) as i32 {
    if (in.head > 0) {
        // Slide the unread tail to the front. At most one partial sequence, or
        // the five bytes a paste holds back in case they are a closing bracket.
        let is i64 as keep = in.tail - in.head;
        if (keep > 0) {
            mem.memcpy(cast(*u8, &in.buf),
                       cast(*u8, cast(i64, &in.buf) + in.head), keep);
        }
        in.head = 0;
        in.tail = keep;
    }
    let is i64 as room with mut = IN_BUF - in.tail;
    if (room <= 0) { return 0; }
    if (want < room) { room = want; }
    if (force == 0) {
        if (_pending(in.fd) == 0) { return 0; }
    }
    let is i64 as n = io._read_fd(in.fd, cast(*u8, cast(i64, &in.buf) + in.tail), room);
    if (n <= 0) { return 0; }
    in.tail = in.tail + n;
    return 1;
}

// Assemble the EV_PASTE between the brackets. Returns 1 when one is ready.
//
// The closing bracket can be split across two reads, so while it has not been
// seen the last five bytes are held back — they might be its beginning. That
// is also why this cannot live in _scan: a paste is the one thing here whose
// event spans more bytes than the reader holds at once.
fn _paste_take(in as *Input, ev as *Event, last as i32) as i32 {
    _ev_clear(ev);
    let is *u8 as b = cast(*u8, &in.buf);
    let is i64 as mark with mut = 0 - 1;
    let is i64 as i with mut = in.head;
    while (i + 6 <= in.tail) {
        if (cast(i64, b[i]) == 27 && cast(i64, b[i + 1]) == 91
            && cast(i64, b[i + 2]) == 50 && cast(i64, b[i + 3]) == 48
            && cast(i64, b[i + 4]) == 49 && cast(i64, b[i + 5]) == 126) {
            mark = i;
            break;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    let is i64 as stop with mut = in.tail;
    if (mark >= 0) { stop = mark; }
    else if (last == 0) {
        stop = in.tail - 5;
        if (stop < in.head) { stop = in.head; }
    }
    let is i64 as take with mut = stop - in.head;
    let is i32 as full with mut = 0;
    let is i64 as room = IN_PASTE - in.plen;
    if (take > room) { take = room; full = 1; }
    if (take > 0) {
        mem.memcpy(cast(*u8, cast(i64, &in.text) + in.plen),
                   cast(*u8, cast(i64, &in.buf) + in.head), take);
        in.plen = in.plen + take;
        in.head = in.head + take;
    }
    if (full == 1) {
        // The cap, reached mid-paste: hand out what we have and keep going.
        ev.kind = EV_PASTE;
        ev.text = cast(*u8, &in.text);
        ev.len = in.plen;
        in.plen = 0;
        return 1;
    }
    if (mark >= 0) {
        in.head = in.head + 6;
        in.inpaste = 0;
        ev.kind = EV_PASTE;
        ev.text = cast(*u8, &in.text);
        ev.len = in.plen;
        in.plen = 0;
        return 1;
    }
    if (last == 1) {
        // A paste whose closing bracket never came. The bytes are real; the
        // bracket is the terminal's problem.
        in.inpaste = 0;
        if (in.plen == 0) { return 0; }
        ev.kind = EV_PASTE;
        ev.text = cast(*u8, &in.text);
        ev.len = in.plen;
        in.plen = 0;
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// The buffer is full and the bytes in it are one unfinished sequence, so no
// read can make progress: the sequence is longer than IN_BUF.
//
// Neither stateless answer is acceptable. Dropping the buffer leaves the tail to
// be parsed as fresh input — 4096 bytes of a hostile writer's choosing, typed
// into whatever has focus, which is the defect this module exists to remove at
// its largest. Keeping the buffer deadlocks the reader. So the rest of the
// sequence is skipped: the reader remembers which terminator it is looking for
// and discards bytes until it arrives. Only a hostile or broken writer gets
// here — the longest sequence this parser recognises is 25 bytes.
fn _skip_begin(in as *Input) as void {
    let is *u8 as b = cast(*u8, &in.buf);
    // Which terminator to look for is read off the first two bytes, and fewer
    // than two bytes cannot say. That is not a state this function was written
    // for — it is called for a buffer FULL of one unfinished sequence — but a
    // default chosen for a string sequence was applied to it anyway, and it
    // reached that state: read_key called this holding a single ESC, the skip
    // went looking for an ST that no terminal was going to send, and every
    // printable byte after it, which is the person's typing, was discarded as
    // string payload. With no sequence here there is nothing to skip: the
    // bytes stay where they are and the caller reads on.
    if (in.tail - in.head < 2) { return; }
    in.skip = 2;                                   // a string: ST or BEL
    if (cast(i64, b[in.head]) == 27 && cast(i64, b[in.head + 1]) == 91) {
        in.skip = 1;                               // a CSI: a final byte
    }
    in.head = in.tail;
}

// Discard buffered bytes until the terminator being skipped to. Clears in.skip
// when it finds it, leaving in.head just past it; leaves in.skip set when the
// buffer runs out first, so the state survives to the next call.
fn _skip_take(in as *Input) as void {
    let is *u8 as b = cast(*u8, &in.buf);
    let is i64 as i with mut = in.head;
    while (i < in.tail) {
        let is i64 as c = cast(i64, b[i]);
        if (in.skip == 1) {
            if (c >= 64 && c <= 126) { in.head = i + 1; in.skip = 0; return; }
            if (c < 32 || c > 126) {
                // Not a byte a CSI can carry: a keystroke arrived instead, so
                // stop skipping and leave it, as _scan does in the same case.
                in.head = i;
                in.skip = 0;
                return;
            }
        } else {
            if (c == 7) { in.head = i + 1; in.skip = 0; return; }        // BEL
            if (c == 27) {
                if (i + 1 >= in.tail) { in.head = i; return; }  // wait for the ST
                if (cast(i64, b[i + 1]) == 92) {                // ESC \ = ST
                    in.head = i + 2;
                    in.skip = 0;
                    return;
                }
                in.head = i;                       // an ESC that is not an ST
                in.skip = 0;
                return;
            }
            if (c < 32) { in.head = i; in.skip = 0; return; }   // a keystroke
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    in.head = in.tail;
}

// The size of the terminal behind a descriptor, or 0 when there is not one.
//
// get_size() stays what it was — fd 0, no Input, no failure reporting — because
// callers use it every frame and its signature is public. This is the internal
// form: it answers about the reader's OWN descriptor, and it says whether the
// answer means anything, which is the part the mouse gate needs.
fn _size_of(fd as i64, rows as *i64, cols as *i64) as i32 {
    if (os.current() == os.OS_LINUX) {
        let is [8]u8 as ws;
        if (linux.ioctl(fd, TIOCGWINSZ, cast(*u8, &ws)) < 0) { return 0; }
        let is *u16 as vals = cast(*u16, &ws);
        *rows = cast(i64, vals[0]);
        *cols = cast(i64, vals[1]);
        if (*rows <= 0 || *cols <= 0) { return 0; }
        return 1;
    } else if (os.current() == os.OS_WINDOWS) {
        // UNVERIFIED, like the rest of the Windows input path: the console size
        // is a property of the OUTPUT handle, not of the reader's descriptor, so
        // this asks about the console the process is attached to.
        let is [22]u8 as info;
        let is i64 as h = win.GetStdHandle(win.STD_OUTPUT_HANDLE);
        if (win.GetConsoleScreenBufferInfo(h, cast(*u8, &info)) == 0) { return 0; }
        let is *i16 as v = cast(*i16, cast(i64, &info) + 10);
        *cols = cast(i64, v[2]) - cast(i64, v[0]) + 1;
        *rows = cast(i64, v[3]) - cast(i64, v[1]) + 1;
        if (*rows <= 0 || *cols <= 0) { return 0; }
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// Is this report describing a cell the terminal actually has?
//
// The coordinates come from outside the process: a mouse report is a short
// preamble and some decimal, and anything with fd 0 open can write one. A caller
// that indexes a grid with row and col — which the struct's own comment invites,
// since they are 1-based as the terminal reports them — writes out of bounds if
// they are larger than the terminal. The parser's _COORD_MAX only rules out
// numbers no terminal could mean; this rules out the ones THIS terminal cannot.
//
// It costs one TIOCGWINSZ per delivered report, measured at 377 ns on this
// machine over a million calls on a real pty. A drag reporting a hundred a
// second therefore costs about 38 microseconds a second, against the ten
// get_size() calls a second the interface on top of this already makes. No cache
// and no refresh policy: a stale size drops real clicks after a resize, which is
// a worse failure than an ioctl.
//
// When the descriptor cannot answer — a pipe, a file, a socket, which is every
// test here and every program with input piped in — there is no size to check
// against, and the parser's ceiling is all there is.
fn _mouse_fits(in as *Input, ev as *Event) as i32 {
    let is i64 as rows with mut = 0;
    let is i64 as cols with mut = 0;
    if (_size_of(in.fd, &rows, &cols) == 0) { return 1; }
    if (ev.row > rows) { return 0; }
    if (ev.col > cols) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}

// Take events out of the buffer until one of them is worth returning. Returns
// 0 when the buffer cannot answer without more bytes.
//
// `assemble` = 1 gathers a bracketed paste into one EV_PASTE, which is what
// input_next wants. read_key passes 0: it answers with an i32, it cannot hand
// out a paste as one thing, and an i32 caller wants the pasted bytes as the
// keystrokes they would have been if typed. With 0 the brackets are consumed
// and reported as nothing and the body flows through byte by byte — which also
// keeps every one of those bytes in the kernel queue until it is read, where a
// level-triggered poller can still see it. Assembling them into this module's
// buffer would hide them from the poller that a caller of read_key is by
// definition using.
fn _drain_buf(in as *Input, ev as *Event, last as i32, assemble as i32) as i32 {
    while (1 == 1) {
        if (in.skip != 0) {
            _skip_take(in);
            if (in.skip != 0) { return 0; }        // the terminator is not here
        }
        if (in.inpaste == 1) {
            if (_paste_take(in, ev, last) == 1) { return 1; }
            return 0;
        }
        let is i64 as avail = in.tail - in.head;
        if (avail <= 0) { return 0; }
        let is i64 as used = _scan(cast(*u8, cast(i64, &in.buf) + in.head),
                                  avail, last, ev);
        if (used <= 0) { return 0; }
        in.head = in.head + used;
        if (ev.kind == EV_KEY) { return 1; }
        if (ev.kind == EV_MOUSE) {
            // A report for a mode this reader never turned on is chatter, not
            // an event. Swallowing it whole is the difference between this
            // reader and the old one. So is a report for a cell the terminal
            // does not have: consumed, and not handed to a caller that would
            // index something with it.
            if (in.m_mouse == 1) {
                if (_mouse_fits(in, ev) == 1) { return 1; }
            }
        } else if (ev.kind == EV_FOCUS) {
            if (in.m_focus == 1) { return 1; }
        } else if (ev.kind == _EV_PASTE_ON) {
            if (in.m_paste == 1 && assemble == 1) { in.inpaste = 1; in.plen = 0; }
        }
        // EV_NONE, a stray closing bracket, a mode nobody asked for: consumed
        // and dropped, and the loop keeps looking for something real.
    }
    return 0;
}

// The reader read_key() speaks to.
//
// It is this module's own, in .bss, and it is the ONLY reader read_key can be
// made to use. The first version of this kept a module-scope pointer to whatever
// Input was last handed to input_open so that an argument-less read_key could
// reach it; a stack-local Input whose frame had returned left that pointer live,
// and the next read_key read and WROTE about 12 KB through it. No pointer into a
// caller's memory can be right here, because read_key is called from code that
// does not know Input exists and cannot say when the object dies.
//
// A program that wants read_key AND input_next against one reader passes this to
// input_open. Opening one of its own instead is allowed and does not break
// anything of its own, but read_key keeps reading THIS one — two buffered
// readers on one descriptor split sequences between them, and the module makes
// the shared one reachable rather than guessing which was meant.
fn shared_input() as *Input {
    if (_live(&_shell) == 0) { _reset(&_shell); }
    return &_shell;
}

// Open the reader on stdin and turn on the modes the caller wants.
//
// It does NOT enter raw mode. Raw mode is a property of the terminal that the
// whole program shares, and the callers that need it already own an
// enter_raw/leave_raw pair around their whole run — flipping it here would
// mean flipping it back in input_close, and closing the reader is not the same
// event as putting the terminal back.
//
// Calling it twice on the same Input is a change of mind about the modes, not a
// fresh start: what the second call no longer wants is put back right there, what
// it already has is not set again, and the record of what is on survives. The
// first version reset that record at the top of every open, which erased the
// first open's undo information — and since input_close is gated on it, mouse
// reporting stayed ON in the terminal after close. Measured: seven sets, two
// resets.
fn input_open(in as *Input, want_mouse as i32) as i64 {
    // Unconditionally, and before the target check, so that "input_open
    // initialises the struct" is true on every path including the refusing one.
    if (in.mark == _MARK_OPEN) { _reset_buf(in); }        // keep the undo record
    else { _reset(in); }
    if (os.current() != os.OS_LINUX && os.current() != os.OS_WINDOWS) {
        return 0 - 1;
    }
    in.mark = _MARK_OPEN;
    if (want_mouse == 1) {
        if (in.m_mouse == 0) {
            // 1000 press/release, 1002 motion while a button is held (drag, which
            // the interface needs for selection and for a draggable scrollbar),
            // 1006 the SGR encoding — the only one whose coordinates are decimal
            // and therefore not capped at column 223.
            io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[?1000h\x1b[?1002h\x1b[?1006h", 24);
            in.m_mouse = 1;
        }
    } else if (in.m_mouse == 1) {
        // A previous open turned these on and this one does not want them: undo
        // them here, in the reverse order of the sets, so the record stays exact.
        io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[?1006l\x1b[?1002l\x1b[?1000l", 24);
        in.m_mouse = 0;
    }
    if (in.m_paste == 0) {
        io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[?2004h", 8);       // bracketed paste
        in.m_paste = 1;
    }
    if (in.m_focus == 0) {
        io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[?1004h", 8);       // focus reporting
        in.m_focus = 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

// Put back every mode input_open turned on, and only those.
//
// This is a requirement, not a finishing touch: a program that dies with mouse
// reporting left on leaves the shell it came from printing `<35;40;12M` at
// every click, and bracketed paste left on leaves it printing `[200~` around
// every paste. The three resets go out in the reverse order of the sets, and
// each one is gated on this reader having actually set it.
fn input_close(in as *Input) as void {
    if (_live(in) == 0) { _reset(in); return; }   // never opened: nothing is on
    if (in.m_focus == 1) {
        io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[?1004l", 8);
        in.m_focus = 0;
    }
    if (in.m_paste == 1) {
        io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[?2004l", 8);
        in.m_paste = 0;
    }
    if (in.m_mouse == 1) {
        io._write_fd(io.STDOUT, "\x1b[?1006l\x1b[?1002l\x1b[?1000l", 24);
        in.m_mouse = 0;
    }
    // Back to initialised-but-not-open. Anything still held — half a sequence, a
    // paste whose closing bracket never arrived — is dropped: the caller has
    // said it is done reading, and there is nobody left to hand it to.
    _reset_buf(in);
    in.mark = _MARK_READY;
}

// The descriptor, for whoever owns the select loop.
//
// Sleeping on it is right; treating its readiness as the answer to "has the
// reader an event" is not — the bytes are in this module's buffer, where no
// epoll set can see them. Drain with input_next until it returns 0 on every
// wake, or the loop runs one event behind and loses the tail of every burst.
fn input_fd(in as *Input) as i64 {
    if (_live(in) == 0) { _reset(in); }
    return in.fd;
}

// One event, or 0 for "drained".
//
// Blocking: it reads only when the descriptor says it has something, EXCEPT
// while it holds an unresolved prefix — a lone ESC, half a mouse report, a
// paste whose closing bracket has not arrived — where it does one blocking
// read, because that wait is the only thing that can tell a bare Escape from
// the start of an arrow key. Under raw mode's VMIN=0/VTIME=1 that read is
// bounded at 100 ms; under a pipe it is bounded by the writer, which is why
// interfaces are tested through a pty and not through a pipe.
fn input_next(in as *Input, ev as *Event) as i32 {
    if (_live(in) == 0) { _reset(in); }
    while (1 == 1) {
        if (_drain_buf(in, ev, 0, 1) == 1) { return 1; }
        let is i32 as force with mut = 0;
        if (in.tail > in.head) { force = 1; }
        if (in.inpaste == 1) { force = 1; }
        if (_fill(in, IN_BUF, force) == 0) {
            if (in.inpaste == 0 && in.tail - in.head >= IN_BUF) {
                // Not "nothing more is coming" — no ROOM for more. The sequence
                // is longer than the buffer; skip the rest of it rather than let
                // its tail be read as keystrokes.
                _skip_begin(in);
                continue;
            }
            // Nothing more is coming: whatever is held has to resolve now.
            if (_drain_buf(in, ev, 1, 1) == 1) { return 1; }
            _ev_clear(ev);
            return 0;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}

// Read a key from stdin. Returns KEY_* constants or an ASCII byte, KEY_NONE if
// there was nothing to report — the same signature and the same answers as
// before, now with the parser underneath shared with input_next.
//
// Two properties are kept deliberately, because a caller depends on each:
//
//   - one call takes ONE sequence off the descriptor and answers as soon as that
//     sequence resolves: a keystroke as its i32, anything that is not one as
//     KEY_NONE. It reads one byte per read(2), exactly as it always did, so the
//     rest of a burst stays in the kernel queue where a level-triggered poller
//     can still see it. Anything else silently breaks a select loop that calls
//     this once per readiness report.
//
//     Three qualifications, all of them measured rather than reasoned:
//
//       Bytes already in THIS module's buffer when the call starts are parsed
//       with no read at all — the ESC ESC pair this reader holds back, or
//       whatever input_next left there, since input_next reads in blocks and
//       this does not. So a run of chatter sitting in that buffer can be
//       consumed inside one call. Nothing is lost by it, but no poller can
//       account for it either, which is why it is written down.
//
//       A sequence longer than _RK_MAX stops being assembled at 64 bytes and is
//       skipped to its terminator instead, across as many following calls as the
//       terminator takes to arrive, KEY_NONE throughout.
//
//       A paste's body is not one sequence but many answers: one byte per call,
//       one read(2) per byte. See the paste paragraph below.
//
//   - the read blocks. VMIN=0/VTIME=1 bounds it at about 100 ms, and that
//     timeout is what makes a bare Escape resolvable at all.
//
// ONE byte does not answer as itself: NUL, which Ctrl+@ and Ctrl+Space produce.
// KEY_NONE is 0 and so is that byte, and KEY_NONE is an answer this function
// gives all the time — so a caller that guards on it, which is every caller,
// drops the keystroke. It is answered with KEY_NUL instead, a code outside both
// the byte range and the [32,1000) window a consumer reads as "a typable byte".
// Nothing else moved: the event API still reports that byte as EV_KEY with
// key=0, where a kind and a key say between them what one i32 could not.
//
// A mouse report or a focus report is not an i32, so it is consumed whole and
// answered with KEY_NONE — the SAME answer whether or not this reader has that
// report's mode on. With the mode off the report is chatter and is dropped; with
// it on the report is a real event an i32 cannot carry, and either way the call
// ends there instead of reading past it.
//
// The two configurations did not answer the same for a while, and the open one
// was the wrong half: with the modes on, ONE call swallowed three SGR reports
// AND the keystroke behind them and answered with the key. Measured under strace,
// the same bytes both ways — 11, 11, 11, 1, 1 reads answering 0, 0, 0, 120, 0
// now, against 34, 1, 1, 1, 1 reads answering 120 first and nothing after.
//
// The old reader could not answer a report this way at all, because it had no way
// to know where a sequence ended. KEY_NONE was already a possible answer (VMIN=0
// lets a read return zero bytes), so every caller already guards on it.
//
// A PASTE IS DIFFERENT, because it is not the terminal talking: it is text a
// person meant to put into the program. Answering KEY_NONE and dropping it
// destroys the user's input, so read_key does not assemble one at all — the
// brackets are consumed and reported as nothing, and the bytes between them
// come out as the keystrokes they would have been if typed, one per call, in
// order, still one byte per read(2). The cost of that choice is stated plainly:
// an ESC inside pasted text is read as an escape sequence, exactly as it would
// be if the user had typed it, because an i32 cannot carry "this byte was
// pasted, not typed". A caller that needs that distinction calls input_next.
//
// If input_next had already begun assembling a paste when read_key is called —
// the mixed configuration this module supports on purpose — read_key stops the
// assembly and reads on. The paste's remaining bytes are in this module's byte
// buffer and come out of there as keystrokes, in order, so nothing is dropped in
// either direction.
//
// What does NOT happen at that moment is a chunk of already-gathered bytes being
// handed over, and this paragraph used to say it did. input_next can only return
// with an assembly still open by way of _paste_take hitting the IN_PASTE cap,
// which has already delivered every byte it gathered; there is never a partial
// chunk sitting in the paste buffer between calls. The code that handed one out
// one byte per call, and the two fields it needed, were unreachable from any
// sequence of public calls — measured with instrumentation, zero hits across the
// whole suite — and are gone.
// The i32 read_key() answers with, for an event that IS a key.
//
// Every value passes through untouched except one: the byte 0, which cannot,
// because 0 is already KEY_NONE on this channel. It comes out as KEY_NUL — see
// that constant for why neither of the alternatives is available, and why the
// event API needs no such translation.
fn _rk_key(k as i32) as i32 {
    if (k == 0) { return KEY_NUL; }       // the NUL byte, not "nothing"
    return k;
}

fn read_key() as i32 {
    let is *Input as in = shared_input();
    let is Event as ev;
    // Bytes read while resolving ONE sequence, and nothing else. It is what
    // _RK_MAX bounds, so anything that makes it mean "work this call has done"
    // charges one sequence for another's bytes — which is exactly how a burst of
    // reports crossed the ceiling inside a single call and took the typing
    // behind it with it. Every path that finishes a sequence returns.
    let is i64 as pulled with mut = 0;
    while (1 == 1) {
        if (in.inpaste == 1) {
            // input_next was assembling a paste through this reader. Stop
            // assembling: read_key cannot answer with an EV_PASTE, and the
            // paste's remaining bytes are in this module's byte buffer, where the
            // loop below reads them as the keystrokes they would have been if
            // typed — in the order the person pasted them.
            //
            // Nothing gathered is waiting here, ever. The only state input_next
            // can RETURN in with an assembly still open is _paste_take reaching
            // the IN_PASTE cap, and that path has already handed every gathered
            // byte out as an EV_PASTE and left plen at 0; every other path clears
            // inpaste on its way out. So plen is 0 on this line, and the version
            // of this that copied plen into a window and handed those bytes out
            // one per call could not run: instrumented, zero hits across the
            // whole suite, and no sequence of public calls reaches it.
            in.inpaste = 0;
            in.plen = 0;
        }
        let is i64 as was = in.head;
        if (_drain_buf(in, &ev, 0, 0) == 1) {
            if (ev.kind == EV_KEY) { return _rk_key(ev.key); }
            // A report for a mode this reader DID turn on: a mouse report, a
            // focus report. It is not an i32, so the call ends here with
            // KEY_NONE — the same answer, and the same cost, as the report that
            // gets dropped below because nothing asked for its mode. Reading on
            // instead is what made the two configurations answer differently: a
            // burst of delivered reports was consumed inside one call, together
            // with the keystroke behind it, and `pulled` counted the whole
            // burst against one sequence's ceiling.
            return KEY_NONE;
        } else {
            // A whole sequence went past and none of it was a key — a report
            // for a mode this reader did not turn on, or a reply the terminal
            // sent unasked — and the buffer is empty behind it, which is always
            // true of bytes this call read itself, one per read(2). It is
            // consumed; answer "nothing" now rather than read on, so that one
            // call still costs one sequence and the rest of a burst stays in the
            // kernel where a poller can see it. When the buffer is NOT empty the
            // bytes came from input_next, which reads in blocks: the loop keeps
            // parsing them, making no read, until something resolves.
            if (in.head > was && in.head == in.tail) { return KEY_NONE; }
            if (pulled >= _RK_MAX) {
                // Bounded: one call must cost about one keystroke, so a sequence
                // longer than 64 bytes is not assembled here. It is SKIPPED to
                // its terminator rather than dropped — dropping it would leave
                // its tail to be read as keystrokes, which is the same defect at
                // a smaller size. Reachable only from a terminal sending
                // something no terminal sends.
                _skip_begin(in);
                return KEY_NONE;
            }
            if (_fill(in, 1, 1) == 0) {
                while (_drain_buf(in, &ev, 1, 0) == 1) {
                    if (ev.kind == EV_KEY) { return _rk_key(ev.key); }
                }
                return KEY_NONE;
            }
            pulled = pulled + 1;
        }
    }
    return KEY_NONE;
}
